Types from PHPStan
******************

.[perex]
DressCode's lossless syntax tree understands every single space and comma in your codebase, but on its own it cannot know that a variable named `$form` represents a form instance. When you have PHPStan installed in your project, DressCode queries it directly. This unlocks capabilities a code style tool does not usually have: rewriting deprecated library APIs based on what a variable actually is, inserting `#[\Override]` on methods overriding parents, and verifying that a string literal safely matches an existing class name.


What the Tree Does Not Know
===========================

A rule inspecting the syntax tree in isolation sees a line accessing the constant `$form::FILLED`. But looking at a single file, it has no way of knowing what `$form` holds at runtime. If it is a Nette form, whose class annotates that constant with `@deprecated use Form::Filled`, the line should be rewritten. If it is any other object, the code must be left completely untouched. An automated fix based on guesswork is far more dangerous than no fix at all.

Static analysis knows the exact answer, and you most likely already run it in your project via PHPStan. DressCode therefore doesn't calculate types on its own. Instead, it queries your existing PHPStan installation, running with your project's `phpstan.neon` and all your configured extensions. The answers are just as dependable as the static analysis you already rely on every day, seamlessly understanding any framework-specific magic your extensions support.


Enabling the Types .[#enable]
=============================

Ideally, both PHPStan and DressCode belong directly in your project dependencies, ensuring DressCode consults the exact version of PHPStan and the configuration your team uses:

```shell
composer require --dev phpstan/phpstan dresscode/dresscode
```

Keep in mind that DressCode itself requires PHP 8.4 or newer to run, so your project must run on it too. The target PHP version your codebase is written for is a completely separate setting and can safely remain lower (see [Installation |getting-started#Installation]).

In your configuration, enabling type analysis takes a single line:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan
```

That is all. The `typeAnalysis` key is a project fact (much like your target PHP version), and presets cannot enable it on your behalf.

DressCode queries the PHPStan installed alongside it, that is, within the same `vendor/` directory. When both are installed in your project dependencies, as shown above, it communicates directly with your project's local PHPStan. Alternatively, you can install both tools globally:

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode phpstan/phpstan
```

A global PHPStan also understands your project's classes and dependencies without needing to load them: it reads structural declarations directly from source files using `composer.json` and `vendor/composer/installed.json`. However, analysis will run on the globally installed PHPStan version rather than the one locked in your project. Any PHPStan extensions must be installed globally as well (via `phpstan/extension-installer`). A global PHPStan does not load files from your project's local `vendor/` directory, meaning extensions registered in `phpstan.neon` via relative vendor paths won't find their classes unless also installed globally. Extensions that require a booted runtime environment (such as Larastan booting a Laravel application) will not function globally at all.

Installing DressCode via `composer create-project` includes PHPStan automatically, as it pulls in DressCode's development dependencies. You then update PHPStan by running `composer update` inside that standalone directory.

If you set `typeAnalysis: phpstan` but no PHPStan installation exists alongside DressCode (for instance, in a global installation where PHPStan was omitted), DressCode issues a warning and proceeds without type analysis, exactly as if the key were absent:

/--pre .[terminal]
Warning: The configuration sets `typeAnalysis: phpstan`, but `phpstan/phpstan` is not installed beside DressCode, so the run goes without the types of the code.
See https://dresscode.run/types#enable
\--

However, if you explicitly configure a decision that strictly requires static types, that run will fail with a configuration error, just as it would [without the `typeAnalysis` key |#Without PHPStan]:

/--pre .[terminal]
Error: Decision `upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember` needs the types of the code, but `phpstan/phpstan` is not installed beside DressCode.
See https://dresscode.run/types#enable
\--

The [`dresscode upgrade today` |upgrade] command enables type analysis automatically, even without the key in the configuration. If it cannot find PHPStan alongside itself, it refuses to run and halts immediately with an error: <code>''The command needs the types of the code, but `phpstan/phpstan` is not installed beside DressCode.''</code>


What You Gain
=============

Take a presenter that uses deprecated Nette APIs and older conventions, checked with the following configuration:

```neon
use:
	- nette
	- deprecations

typeAnalysis: phpstan

upgrading:
	classes:
		Override: required
```

The message of each finding is in a comment on its line:

```php .[before]
final class SignPresenter extends Presenter
{
	protected function startup(): void  // The method overriding `Nette\Application\UI\Presenter::startup()` must be marked with `#[\Override]`.
	{
		parent::startup();
		$this->setLayout('sign');
	}


	protected function createComponentSignInForm(): Form
	{
		$form = new Form;
		$form->addText('name')
			->setAttribute('autofocus')  // Method `Nette\Forms\Controls\BaseControl::setAttribute()` is deprecated: use setHtmlAttribute().
			->addRule($form::FILLED, 'Enter your name');  // Constant `Nette\Forms\Form::FILLED` is deprecated: use Form::Filled.
		return $form;
	}


	public function isAnonymous(string $name): bool
	{
		return strlen($name) === 0;  // Useless `strlen()`, because `=== ''` says the same.
	}


	public function getRepositoryClass(): string
	{
		return 'App\Model\UserRepository';  // The class name must be written `App\Model\UserRepository::class` instead of a string.
	}
}
```

And here is the file after running automated fixes:

```php .[after]
final class SignPresenter extends Presenter
{
	#[Override]
	protected function startup(): void
	{
		parent::startup();
		$this->setLayout('sign');
	}


	protected function createComponentSignInForm(): Form
	{
		$form = new Form;
		$form->addText('name')
			->setHtmlAttribute('autofocus')
			->addRule($form::Filled, 'Enter your name');
		return $form;
	}


	public function isAnonymous(string $name): bool
	{
		return $name === '';
	}


	public function getRepositoryClass(): string
	{
		return App\Model\UserRepository::class;
	}
}
```

Every single one of these five findings required knowing something that cannot be deduced from the file's syntax alone. A sixth report that `dresscode check` displays is merely a downstream consequence of the first: in a file without a namespace, the `nette` preset writes global names without a leading backslash, converting `#[\Override]` into clean `#[Override]`.

- **Deprecated APIs** are updated by `upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember` based on `@deprecated` annotations in library source code. Resolution is driven by the class that actually declares the constant or method rather than how it was invoked in code: `$form::FILLED` is rewritten identically to `Form::FILLED`, and the message for `setAttribute()` accurately cites the `BaseControl` class even though it was called on a text input control. Deprecated classes are updated similarly via `upgrading.declarations.deprecatedClass`. The maps driving library upgrades (`upgrading.api.replacedMembers`, `replacedCalls`, `forbiddenMembers`, and `nette.flagsAsNamedArguments`) also rely fundamentally on types, and `upgrading.api.replacedClasses` uses them to ensure that newly written class names genuinely exist in the project (see [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries]).
- **The `#[\Override]` attribute** from PHP 8.3 is added by `upgrading.classes.Override: required` to any method that overrides a method in a parent class. PHP will then throw a compile-time fatal error if the parent ever renames that method, preventing your method from silently drifting into dead code. Implementations of abstract methods or interface contracts are intentionally skipped, as PHP already enforces those strictly without attributes.
- **Class names in string literals** are safely converted to `::class` syntax by `literals.classNameInString` only when a class, interface, or enum of that exact name genuinely exists in the project. IDEs will then reliably catch the reference during future refactorings. According to its own documentation, PHP CS Fixer's `class_keyword` rule cannot provide this safety because a token-based linter cannot know what classes exist across your project.
- **Empty string comparisons** like `strlen($name) === 0` are rewritten to `$name === ''` by `hygiene.strlenEmptyTest` only when static analysis confirms that `$name` is definitively a string. For any other type, the two constructs could behave differently.

Type information also powers `classes.overridingMethod.parentCallOnly`, which deletes boilerplate methods that do nothing except pass arguments to the parent method of the same name. Static analysis verifies whether callers could tell the difference: the parent must declare identical visibility, parameters, and return types. Because a parent method calling `func_get_args()` would receive extra arguments and one call frame would vanish from stack traces, this fix is risky; DressCode makes it once you list the decision in the `fixRisky` key.

Decisions that safeguard inheritance contracts after upgrading libraries depend on types as well: `classes.overridingMethod.signature` adapts a child method to a parent's updated signature, `mistakes.finalParent` flags child classes extending a class that recently became `final`, and `mistakes.unimplementedAbstractMethod` detects an abstract method the child does not implement. In the `dresscode/upgrade-nette` package, `nette.linkArguments` and `nette.monitorCallbacks` rely on types similarly.

Many other decisions run without types, but become significantly more capable with them. Fixes that would otherwise be classified as risky due to unknown types can be applied safely: `upgrading.functions.arraySearchFunctions` confirms that a loop iterates over an array, leaving loops over objects alone; decisions like `expressions.comparison.loose`, `upgrading.syntax.match`, and `mistakes.strictArgument` verify that operands are integers, booleans, or enum cases (where loose and strict comparisons produce identical results); `expressions.assignment.addingOne` confirms numeric values; `upgrading.functions.get_debug_type` recognizes a string or an array; `expressions.assignment.targetInValue` verifies standard properties that `??=` can safely leave unwritten; and `expressions.coalesce` confirms that property or array access bypasses magic methods. The `upgrading.syntax.pipe` decision verifies that methods don't take parameters by reference (without types, piping method calls is risky). The `classes.staticMethod` decision accurately determines true ancestors. Decisions like `upgrading.api.attributeForMember` (and `laravel.castsMethod` and `laravel.scopeAttribute` from `dresscode/upgrade-laravel`) recognize without types only a class that names the parent in `extends` or `implements`; with types, also one that inherits it across intermediary base classes (such as models extending your custom `BaseModel`). With types, `upgrading.api.attributeForMember` also detects inherited interfaces and flags attributes missing mandatory constructor arguments. Attribute validation with types similarly flags missing arguments in `upgrading.api.attributeForAnnotation` and reports target classes that don't exist or aren't attributes when namespace wildcards are used.

You don't need to enable type-dependent decisions individually: they are curated into the `cleanup`, `deprecations`, and `modernizations` [sets |configuration#Standards and Sets], and configuring `typeAnalysis` automatically provides them with data. Do not confuse the set with the key: the `types` set dictates that you want to actively declare types in code, while the `typeAnalysis` key specifies where DressCode learns about the types that already exist. The `nette` standard already enforces code hygiene, so you only need to supply the key and attach `deprecations` and `modernizations` (which are omitted from standards because upgrading is a step you run when you choose):

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- nette
	- deprecations
	- modernizations
```

With any other standard, you layer the sets alongside it:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- perCs
	- cleanup
	- deprecations
	- modernizations
```


How They Work Together
======================

DressCode never abandons its lossless syntax tree to obtain static types. It prints the tree to memory, lets PHPStan parse and analyze the code, and attaches each deduced type directly to its corresponding node based on source coordinates. Rules then query the node directly: What object is being called? Which method does this access target? What does its signature declare? PHPStan rewrites nothing and reports nothing; it acts as an oracle of facts. All actual refactoring and formatting is performed by DressCode, keeping all original indentation, whitespace, and comments intact.

This analysis involves overhead: every file passes through two separate parsers, and PHPStan must infer types. Running with `typeAnalysis: phpstan` is therefore noticeably slower than a purely syntactic check. However, results are cached, so you will primarily feel the performance impact on the initial run or after a configuration change.

The cache operates on individual file contents rather than the broader project state. If a method in one of your classes is annotated with `@deprecated`, files calling that method have not changed on disk, so the cache will skip them until they are touched. Similarly, the cache cannot detect changes made to `phpstan.neon`. It does, however, invalidate automatically when libraries, PHPStan, or its extensions are updated, as their versions form part of the cache fingerprint. After making changes that the cache cannot observe, run DressCode with `--no-cache`. In CI environments where builds pass or fail, always run checks with types enabled and cache disabled.


Without PHPStan
===============

Without the `typeAnalysis` key, DressCode functions normally; it simply omits decisions that require static types. When those decisions come from a preset, a set or the upgrade data of a package, they are skipped quietly, allowing you to use presets like `nette` (which contains several type-dependent decisions) even in codebases where PHPStan is absent. Running `dresscode config` explains the reason beside each skipped decision:

/--pre .[terminal]
classes:
	overridingMethod:
		parentCallOnly: forbidden               # dresscode/cleanup, no effect: types
...
hygiene:
	strlenEmptyTest: forbidden                  # dresscode/cleanup, no effect: types
...
literals:
	classNameInString: forbidden                # dresscode/cleanup, no effect: types
\--

However, if you explicitly configure a type-dependent decision in your own configuration file, DressCode flags it as a configuration error. You requested behavior the engine cannot deliver, and running without it would falsely report that your code is clean:

/--pre .[terminal]
Error: Decision `upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember` needs the types of the code; set `typeAnalysis: phpstan` in the configuration and install `phpstan/phpstan` beside DressCode.
See https://dresscode.run/types#enable
\--


